# Release Policy

Landing a change and releasing it are separate decisions. The default after
finishing work is **no tag**: add the entry to `## [Unreleased]` in
`CHANGELOG.md`, commit, and stop there.

## Numbering

Semantic versioning. While the project is `0.x`, MINOR carries breaking changes.

| Change | Bump |
|---|---|
| A user-visible feature | MINOR (`0.5.0` → `0.6.0`) |
| A bug fix or wording change | PATCH (`0.5.0` → `0.5.1`) |

## When to cut one

Tag only when one of these holds, and name which one when proposing it:

- A user-visible feature is complete and documented.
- Fixes have piled up in `Unreleased` and the last release was on an earlier day.
- **Hotfix**: the released version is broken. This is the only case that allows a
  second release on the same day.

v0.4.0 through v0.5.0 shipped in one afternoon, and two of those releases existed
only to clean up bugs the previous one introduced. Accumulating would have
produced a single good release.

## Before tagging

1. `make check` is green.
2. `make build`, then actually play the binary: open cells, flag, finish a game,
   and exercise whatever changed. Green tests did not catch a menu that could not
   be opened from the game-over screen.
3. `CHANGELOG.md` has an `Unreleased` section with real entries. Rename it to the
   version being cut.
4. The code is already committed and pushed, and was **not written in this same
   turn**. Verify first, tag later.

## Never

- Tag to make a task feel finished. An unreleased change on `main` is finished.
- Write a CHANGELOG entry from memory of the diff instead of reading it.
